                        <slur xml:id="ue1937-III-slur-L122F1-L123F1N1" staff="2" startid="#ue1937-III-note-L122F1" endid="#ue1937-III-note-L123F1S2" curvedir="above" />
                        <slur xml:id="ue1937-III-slur-L121F2-L123F1N2" type="placed" staff="1" startid="#ue1937-III-note-L121F2" endid="#ue1937-III-chord-L123F1" curvedir="below" />
                        <annot xml:id="ue1937-III-annot-m015-grace-slur" type="editorial" label="encoding-note" plist="#ue1937-III-slur-L122F1-L123F1N1 #ue1937-III-note-L122F1 #ue1937-III-note-L123F1S2" facs="#zone-ue1937-III-m015" resp="#resp.editor" source="#src.ue1937">
                           <p>UE p. 8 prints the short E-natural4 grace-note slur below, ending on the upper G-natural4 (L123F1S2), not the lower G-sharp3 or the whole chord. Verovio 6.3.0-425dd7b draws the lower curve beneath the whole chord even with this note-specific endid; the same behaviour occurs without the longer cross-staff slur. As an editor-approved rendering workaround, curvedir="above" associates the curve with the upper note. The note-specific endpoint is retained for analysis; the printed lower direction is recorded here. The longer cross-staff slur is unchanged. See <ref target="https://github.com/rism-digital/verovio/issues/4313">Verovio issue 4313</ref>.</p>
                        </annot>
